{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall not oppress your neighbor.</b> In secret." "<b>Nor shall you rob.</b> Openly, by force." "<b>The hired laborer's wage.</b> Like \"hiring\" [שכירות]; so too \"and his deed is before him\" [ופעולתו]. It is possible that it is a shortened form, the meaning being \"the wage of his labor\" [שכר פעולתו]. Many interpreted it as referring to a day laborer: they say to him, \"You shall also work in the morning, and in the morning I will give you the wages of two days.\" The translators said that it is a day laborer, for a night laborer — the sun does not set upon him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "The final clause is hyper-literal; Ibn Ezra's argument hinges on the technical distinction between a day laborer (שכיר יום) and a night laborer (שכיר לילה) and how the phrase לא תלין פעולת שכיר אתך עד בקר applies to each. 'The translators' (המעתיקים) likely refers to the Targum Onkelos and/or other ancient versions."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:10:59.525868885Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}